# Independent review: remote acknowledgement during recorded status wait

Verdict: no blocking finding in the reviewed remote-wait scope. Exact final sources are under source/; the implementation diff was reviewed against base 2a4183c.

Compared journal.wait_remote, the new internal callbacks, tests and README with chapter 15's journal durability/order requirements and the existing pinned-origin normal-fast-forward transport. An absolute wait deadline is captured before the initial remote head check. The bridge confirms the supplied head, validates local receipt/history, appends each response locally using CAS, and publishes/acknowledges that exact child commit before another fetch or a successful return. Local history validation and remote work consume the same deadline. Callback failures propagate without a retry; the already saved local response remains available for reconciliation.

Independent execution: three new real-bare-Git remote-wait tests and eight existing receipt/status/local-wait integration tests passed normally and with Python -O. The tests verify remote observation presence before the next request, successful final remote head/byte equality, interrupted remote acknowledgement preserving local evidence and refusing silent restart, and initial remote confirmation exhausting the shared budget. An additional real-Git probe performs an actual observation push/ack and then simulates 61 seconds elapsed against a 60-second budget. The result is DEADLINE with one fetch, identical acknowledged local/remote heads, and the original success response retained remotely. Raw outputs and command records preserve execution.

The remote-error integration test injects failure at remote.publish; it does not simulate every network failure after server acceptance. Earlier remote transport behavior is reused rather than replaced. All servers here are temporary local bare Git repositories; API responses and elapsed-time edges are deterministic test inputs. No live GitHub status request or NEPL3 remote journal write occurred.

The README correctly leaves lock ownership, protection checks, reconciliation of unmatched heads and interpretation of terminal observations to the publisher. Git subprocess limits do not provide hard realtime interruption of an operation already running. Remote acknowledgement proves the observed journal head at that check, not current public deployment identity, smoke success, rollback eligibility or LKG promotion. The reviewer made no implementation edits.
